# Sprint 28a (1b, M13) — the aborts Windows is ALLOWED to have, by REASON.
#
# Each line is a substring; every abort the suite reports must match one.
# An abort matching nothing FAILS the job.
#
# PROVENANCE: MEASURED on CI run 31852349674, the first Windows run of this
# branch. The list started as patterns derived from the source's assumption
# messages; three more were added from what Windows actually reported, which is
# the gate working as intended on its first outing.

# The embedding corpus is a large artifact not carried in the repository.
no corpus at -Djawata.embed.corpus

# The profiler ranks SAMPLED measurements; under contention it can return too
# few samples to rank anything, and refuses to assert on a coin toss.
CPU profile too thin

# The RCP fixture stands in for a NATIVE eclipse launcher. A .cmd cannot exec
# the way the POSIX fake does, so the held JVM sits behind a cmd.exe the attach
# wait never times out on — the first attempt burned 75 runner-minutes before
# CI killed it (run 31789882384). An honest skip beats that.
the RCP launcher fake needs exec semantics a Windows .cmd cannot express

# A .cmd stand-in does not receive argv intact under ProcessBuilder's cmd.exe
# wrapping: the batch twin scans its whole argv for the -Dmdep.outputFile
# prefix and exits 3 on a miss, which run 31804158929 shows exactly. The
# sibling tests needing NO argv pass on Windows, so the invocation path itself
# is proven there. The java-launcher fake that removes this skip is M11.
the .cmd fake does not receive argv under cmd.exe wrapping

# --- MEASURED on the first Windows run (CI 31852349674) ---

# The matrix jobs deliberately set -Djawata.skip.gradle=true; the separate
# gradle-cell job is where these must NOT skip. See expected-aborts.linux.
Gradle Tooling API tests skipped via jawata.skip.gradle=true

# Native crash triage parses hs_err in the POSIX dialect. Windows writes a
# different one (EXCEPTION_ACCESS_VIOLATION, a .mdmp beside the log), and the
# tests say so rather than asserting against text they cannot read. The
# dialect itself is DEFERRED to the Sprint-28-family native-triage batch; the
# boundary already places its seams (HostFs.crashLogs keeps the minidump out
# of the hs_err set), so the feature slots in without moving them.
hs_err parsing speaks the POSIX dialect

# A guard test needs a directory the process cannot read. The Windows runner's
# user ignores the read bit, so the precondition cannot be established — and
# the test refuses to pass a guard it never actually exercised.
cannot make a directory unreadable

# mcp#26's DECLARING-PROJECT half. SourceIsAttributedToItsDeclarerTest asks which
# project a type is attributed to when one project RESOLVES a type another
# DECLARES — and it aborts rather than passing when the fixture cannot pose that
# question, in its own words: "a green line here would say 'the attribution is
# right' when what happened is that no project ever claimed a type it does not
# own, so the loop was never asked to choose." The kind="src"
# path="/xref-declarer" reference does not put the shared type on xref-consumer's
# classpath, so the condition never arises here.
#
# Acceptable because refusing an unearned green is the behaviour we want, and the
# SIBLING case in the same class DOES run, covering the issue's other half (the
# payload naming the declaring FILE). The cost is stated rather than hidden: on
# this fixture the declaring-project assertion is unverified.
#
# Budgeted at Sprint 28e Stage 5 (mcp#45). It had been aborting in every local run
# and nothing reported it, because run-suite.sh never called this gate — which is
# exactly the defect mcp#45 filed.
[mcp#26 ATTRIBUTION] NOT RUN

# mcp#18 — A REPRODUCTION OF AN OPEN DEFECT, deliberately off.
#
# probe_clear can return SUCCESS while leaving the target's thread suspended.
# The test catches it, and it is INTERMITTENT: measured firing once in three
# full suite runs on 2026-09-09. In the gating suite it turns a tracked open
# issue into an intermittent build failure, and then every future red is
# ambiguous — the reader cannot tell a regression from #18 firing again.
#
# Acceptable because the coverage is not lost, only moved out of the gate:
# -Djawata.probe.race=true reproduces it exactly as before. The cost is stated
# rather than hidden: until #18 is fixed, nothing in a normal run will notice
# if probe_clear gets WORSE.
#
# DELETE THIS ENTRY when #18 is fixed. A green run then means something.
[mcp#18] NOT RUN

# The D5 unreadable-tree tests build a directory the process CANNOT READ, to prove
# that a tree which cannot be walked is reported as UNKNOWN rather than as empty —
# "a tree that could not be COUNTED does not answer 0, which means removed". They
# construct that state with POSIX permission bits.
#
# Windows has no POSIX permissions, so the state cannot be built and the tests say
# so and abort, rather than passing over a case they never created. Five of them:
# the count, the existence check, the real-orphan control, the artifact size, and
# the caller's row.
#
# Acceptable because the alternative is worse in both directions — a green line
# would claim the unreadable case was checked when no unreadable directory ever
# existed, and deleting the tests would lose the coverage on the two platforms that
# CAN build it. Linux and macOS run all five.
#
# The cost, stated: on Windows the unreadable-tree behaviour is UNVERIFIED. Closing
# it would need the ACL equivalent (DENY on the current user), which is a different
# mechanism and a separate piece of work.
no POSIX permissions here
